    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d293">

                <pb facs="0405" n="394" sameAs="frus:frus1937v02/pg394"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.4061 Road Back/13</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dieckhoff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-06-09">June 9, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: With reference to the
                    conversation which we had yesterday, I should like to point out that, as soon as
                    the talk between Messieurs Dunn and Thomsen had taken place on April 19, my
                    predecessor Dr. Luther instructed the German Consul in Los Angeles to refrain
                    from issuing further warnings to American citizens in connection with the
                    production of plays which we regard as unfriendly towards Germany.</p>
                <p>I now take pleasure in informing you that my Government fully endorses the
                    instruction given by my predecessor and, consequently, I hope we may consider
                    the incident as satisfactorily settled.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dieckhoff</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
